// THE CLOSED ROSTER OF WITNESS IDENTITIES GRANDFATHERED AT THE CUT COMMIT (operator ruling via
// fierce-lark-661, 2026-09-13). It exists so the required floor can carry TWO ceilings without
// either of them being a heuristic: an identity in this roster is judged against the 500ms-
// equivalent budget it was living under, and an identity NOT in it is judged against the
// 100ms-equivalent budget for new work.
//
// IT IS A MONOTONE DEBT CONTRACT IN THE SENSE DESIGN SECTION 5 REQUIRES, AND ALL THREE OF ITS
// CONDITIONS ARE MET RATHER THAN ASSERTED. (i) The subject universe is INDEPENDENTLY DISCOVERED
// AND CLOSED: it is not authored, it is the required floor's own discovery producer output at the
// cut commit -- every identity the floor PLANNED, read from the run's `required_floor_disposition`
// artifact (dispositions `planned` and `planned_as_changed_witness`). (ii) Membership is checked
// AT IDENTITY GRAIN, never by count -- `floor_grandfathered_holds` compares whole qualified
// identities, and the size below is a producer rather than a literal precisely so no count can
// stand in for the join. (iii) ROWS MAY ONLY LEAVE. A removal carries a typed disposition; an
// ADDITION IS REFUSED, which is what makes this a debt that shrinks rather than a list that grows.
//
// WHY A GROWING ROSTER WOULD DESTROY THE MECHANISM, stated because it is the whole point: the new
// tier only bites if new work cannot join the old tier. A roster that admitted rows would let every
// expensive new witness be grandfathered on the day it landed, which is the escape hatch DESIGN
// section 5 forbids -- a toggle whose only effect is "proceed as if the refusal had not fired".
//
// A RENAME IS A DELETION PLUS AN ADDITION, so a renamed witness is NEW. That is a consequence of
// identity-grain membership rather than a rule layered on top of it, and it is deliberate: it
// removes the one move that would otherwise launder an expensive grandfathered witness into a
// fresh identity while keeping its tier.
//
// NOTHING HERE IS HAND-TYPED. The rows are generated once, from the producer named above, at the
// commit this lane lands on. A later edit that wants to change the population must change what the
// floor discovers, not this file.

// THE TYPED DISPOSITION A REMOVAL CARRIES. DESIGN section 5: a monotone debt contract is legitimate
// only when "every removal carries a typed disposition" -- an untyped shrink is indistinguishable
// from a row quietly going missing, which is the state the contract exists to make impossible.
//
// THERE IS NO `RemovedForConvenience` ARM AND THERE MAY NOT BE. Each arm names a fact that is
// checkable against the tree or against a run: the witness is gone, its identity changed, or it was
// measured inside the new-witness budget and no longer needs the older ceiling.
type GrandfatheredRemoval =
    WitnessDeleted { identity: NonEmptyStr, deleted_in: NonEmptyStr }
  | WitnessRenamed { previous_identity: NonEmptyStr, new_identity: NonEmptyStr, renamed_in: NonEmptyStr }
  | WitnessNowInsideNewTier { identity: NonEmptyStr, observed_eval_steps: Int, measured_by: NonEmptyStr }

// EMPTY AT THE CUT, AND THAT IS THE HONEST STATE RATHER THAN A PLACEHOLDER. The roster is minted at
// this commit, so nothing has left it yet. The carrier is declared now because the contract is
// declared now: a removal path that arrives later, beside the first removal, is a path nobody
// reviewed against the contract it is supposed to satisfy.
data floor_grandfathered_removals: List<GrandfatheredRemoval> = []

